# Authentication And Permissions

Authentication means knowing who the user is.

Permissions mean knowing what the user is allowed to access.

They are different.

## Authentication

Authentication answers:

```text
Who are you?
```

Examples:

```text
Login with email and password
Login with Google
Login with GitHub
JWT token
Session cookie
```

## Current Authentication

The current backend uses:

```text
email and password registration
email and password login
JWT token authentication
protected API routes
```

Simple login result:

```js
{
  userId: "user_001",
  email: "admin@example.com",
  role: "admin"
}
```

## Permissions

Permissions answer:

```text
What can you access?
```

Example:

```text
Admin can see all company PDFs.
HR can see employee policy PDFs.
Employee can see public handbook PDFs.
Manager can see documents for their branch.
```

## Why This Matters For RAG

RAG must only retrieve documents the user is allowed to see.

Bad example:

```text
Employee asks: What is the CEO salary?
Retriever searches private finance documents.
```

Good example:

```text
Employee asks: What is the CEO salary?
Retriever only searches documents the employee can access.
If the answer is not allowed, the chatbot does not reveal it.
```

## Simple Permission Model

Document record:

```js
{
  id: "doc_001",
  fileName: "morocco-salaries.pdf",
  accessLevel: "hr_only"
}
```

User record:

```js
{
  id: "user_001",
  role: "employee"
}
```

Rule:

```text
employee cannot access hr_only documents
```

## Current Permission Model

The project now uses:

```text
admin role
normal user role
team owner role
team admin role
team member role
```

Document access levels:

```text
private: only owner/admin can access
team: document owner/admin and members of the selected team can access
public: any logged-in user can access
```

Team rules:

```text
owner/admin can invite members
owner/admin can cancel pending invites
owner/admin can remove members
member can view shared team documents
```

Invitations:

```text
Users can invite an email before that person has an account.
The invited user sees the invitation after register/login.
The invited user can accept the invitation.
The team owner/admin can cancel pending invitations.
```

The frontend now shows:

```text
current team role badge
permission card
disabled invite controls for members
document access filters: All, Private, Team, Public
```

## Simple Presentation Version

```text
Authentication identifies the user. Permissions decide which private, team, or public documents that user can search. Retrieval, sharing, invites, and team actions all respect these permissions.
```
